#set page(numbering: "1 of 1", number-align: right)


#align(center)[
  #text(1.25em)[*Physically based Neural Volumetric Sampling using Instant Neural Graphics Primitives*]

  #text(1.2em)[Elijah Villarmia]

  // Using a block with width constrains the abstract for better readability
  #text(1em)[
    *Abstract*
  ]

  #text(10pt, spacing: 90%)[
    This report presents the motivation, design, training and evaluation of an Instant NGP network integrated into the volumetric scattering kernel of a physically based path tracer (PBRT-v4). It will explore the emergence of Neural Architectures in Rendering, briefly explain Physically Based Path Tracing and how this project integrates the network into the pipeline. We will also explore the data preparation for the model, evaluation of potential model hyper-parameters, model evaluation and integration into the pipeline, as well as the many troubles and findings made.
  ]
]

#set heading(numbering: "1.1.1")


= Introduction

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Motivation
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Rendering: Time vs Realism
]

#p(3)[
  Figuring out a graphics model to render 3D scenes to a 2D screen (or 3D virtual environment for VR/AR) in a realistic manner is an evolving field, with many pipelines, models and approximations being invented in order to solve what is known as *The Rendering Equation*, whilst trying to find a compromise between computation time and realism. Real-time applications typically prioritize computation time in order to present a target number of frames per second (FPS) and typically favours Rasterization which convert a scene's representation of triangles and vertices and converts those into screen space pixels. This achieves speed but at the cost of realism as they only approximate light. In order to get realism, this requires modelling more closely how light travels, which is the goal of Physically based path tracers.
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Neural Rendering
]

#p(3)[
  In the advent of Neural Networks, we have seen them being used in increasing number of fields, from Computer Vision, Language Processing and Image/Video Generation. Given enough clean data and sufficiently complex networks, they have been shown to be able to drastically improve both speed and efficiency of conventional classification and regression methods. This is no different for rendering, where neural network usage ranges from improving current pipelines, to recreating scenes from a collection of images or video to render an interactive world.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Problem Statement: Volume Rendering
]

#p(2)[
  Current solutions for rendering volumetric effects fall into two extremes. On the one hand, real-time applications perform ray marching approximations which compromise quality for speed. Offline physically based path tracers on the other hand provide photorealism at the cost of computation time from sampling hundreds of thousands of rays per pixel (and subpixel). At the same time, the field of Neural Rendering has introduced state of the art tools such as Neural Radiance fields (NeRFs) to render novel views of scenes, but these are often stand alone and require effort to integrate into a path tracer as well as the fact that many of these networks haven't been designed to approximate volumetric effects. This project attempts to bridge this gap and investigate the feasibility of whether a volumetric scattering GPU kernel can be replaced in a physically based path tracer with Neural Architecture.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Project Objectives
]<Project_Objectives>

#p(2)[
  Though there are many implementations for Path Tracers, almost all rely on sampling rays and estimation in order to calculate The Rendering Equation and sampling a limited set of ray paths per pixel to trace around the scene. Particularly for Volumes which need sampling throughout the medium, this can be costly to imitate fully. One notable path tracer, PBRT-v4, tackles volumetric scattering through this same sampling strategy via a GPU kernel. The goal of this project is to explore the convergence of neural networks with physically based path tracing by integrating Instant Neural Graphics Primitives (Instant-NGP) architecture into PBRT-v4's path tracing system.


  The primary goals are to:
  #list(
    [*Construct a Hybrid Pipeline*: Modify PBRT-v4 source code to extract data directly from the medium scattering kernel],
    [*Train a Neural Network*: Utilize Instant-NGP architecture to learn outputs of the medium scattering GPU kernel in order to approximate RGB emitted radiance $L_e$ and RGB Transmittance $T$ ],
    [*Neural Network Integration*: Hook up the Neural Network into PBRT-v4's ecosystem in order to replace the medium scattering kernel],
    [*Evaluate Performance*: Assess the viability of replacing the medium scattering kernel to generate novel views. Evaluate the network's performance in generating those views according to:
      #list(
        [Visual quality],
        [Computation time],
        [Memory usage],
        indent: 1em,
      )
    ],
  )


]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Milestones in Neural Rendering
]

#p(2)[
  The justification for replacing a path tracing GPU kernel with a Neural Network is rooted in recent advancements in Neural Rendering. These advancements in scene representation and scene encoding has shown that Neural Networks do well in approximating light transport

  #list(
    [
      #text(size: 11pt)[*Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs)*]\
      Introduced by @mildenhall2020nerfrepresentingscenesneural Mildenhall et al, NeRF proved that scenes can be represented by continuous functions of spatio-directional coordinates to color and density, showing networks can learn high-frequency vertex data.
    ],
    [
      #text(size: 11pt)[*Instant Neural Graphics Primitives (Instant-NGP)*]\
      Müller et al @nvidia_instant_ngp addressed performance bottlenecks of NeRF by replacing deep MLPs that learned that scene data with a multi-resolution hash encoding. This architecture allows for near-instant inference of feature vectors, which will be important given the number of required paths queried in PBRT-v4
    ],
    [
      #text(size: 11pt)[*Neural Radiance Caching (NRC)*]\
      Müller et al @NRC technique demonstrated that relatively small MLPs can be trained "_online_" during rendering to cache radiance, showcasing that inference can be fast enough to run within a path tracing loop
    ],
    [
      #text(size: 11pt)[*SeaThru-NeRF*]\
      Slightly different to those above, a group of reasearchers @SeaThru_Nerf created an architecture that explicitly separated an "object" and "medium" network, setting the precedence of separating surface and volume scattering into their own networks in order to handle participating media like fog or water.
    ],
    spacing: 1em,
  )
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Another Notable Architecture
]

#p(2)[
  There are other notable architectures which have been put forward in recent times, but with which we do not consider are given below (including the reason for its absence):\
  \
  #list([
    #text(size: 11pt)[*Gaussian Splatting*]\

    Gaussian Splatting represents a signficant divergence compared to previous encodings, as it represents the scene using anisotropic (directionally heterogeneous) 3D Gaussians (3DGS). Unliked the above architectures that use radiance transfer, it takes inspiration from traditional approaches by utilizing differentiable rasterization as opposed to volumetric ray-marching allowing for real-time rendering speeds and quick training times. \
    \
    However, the reason we did not pursue this representation, although it has been shown to have faster training and visual quality compared to other approaches, is that its scene reperesentation is completely different to what would be expected or would fit in a path tracer as it relies on alpha-blend compositing rather than physically based radiance transfer. Its reliance on a rasterization-based pipeline is incompatible for a physically based path tracer like PBRT-v4, particularly for simulating volumetric scattering for participating media which requires solving the integro-differential equation of transfer.
  ])
]


#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Theoretical Background
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Physically Based Path Tracing (PBRT-v4)
]<PBRT_section>

#p(3)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Conventional rasterization approaches render scenes by utilizing shader programs running in a pipeline, used in real-time applications (such as game engines and modelling software) which takes scene geometry in world space, projects them into render space and converts them into pixel fragments. Lighting calculations and occlusion testing is performed on these fragments to produce the final pixel colour. This process typically has clever shortcuts or techniques to increase speed but may not produce photorealism.
]

#p(3)[

  Other methods such as ray tracing were created which model light closer to real life, by constructing rays that shoot from the camera or lights to calculate scene lighting. Applications that want to simulate more realistic lighting effects at the cost of performance use a Whitted Ray Tracing approach, but this is usually only an enhancement for the rasterization process.

  Path tracing is an evolution in the ray tracing approach. It simulates how light actually propagates, by tracing "paths" from the camera that bounce around the scene, considering both direct illumination from light sources and indrect illumination from surface reflection as well as atteunation or emission effects from participating mediums. It uses mathematical models about how light interacts with surfaces and mediums and is the integral process within path tracing. Ray tracing is the umbrella term, whereas path tracing is a type of ray tracing which physically simulates tracing random light paths for more accurate indirect lighting for photorealism. PBRT-v4 @Pharr:2024:PBRT is one such Path tracer that has risen to prominence.
]



#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  The Rendering Equation
]


#p(3)[
  In Path tracing, as well as rasterization, what we want to solve is known as *The Rendering Equation*. This is an equation which details how much light a point $p$ on a surface emits in direction $omega_o$:

]
$
  L_o (p, omega_o) = L_e (p, omega_o) + integral_(S^2) f(p, omega_o, omega_i) L_i (p, omega_i) |cos theta| d omega_i
$\
#p(3)[

  whose terms are
  #list(
    indent: 2em,
    [$L_o (p, omega_o)$: the outgoing radiance from $p$ in direction $omega_o$],
    [$L_e (p, omega_o)$: the emitted radiance from $p$ in direction $omega_o$],
    [$L_i (p, omega_i)$: the incident radiance at $p$ from all directions on sphere $S^2$],
    [$theta$ is angle between incident direction $omega_i$ and $omega_o$],
    [$f(p, omega_o, omega_i)$ is the Bidirectional Scattering Distribution Function (BSDF) of the surface at $p$],
  )
]

#p(3)[

  This equation can be further broken down into *Surface Scattering* and *Volume Scattering* as both model light scattering differently.
]

// #heading(depth: 3)[
//   #set text(size: 10pt)
//   Surface Scattering
// ]


// #p(3)[
//   The *Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Function (BRDF)* describes how light is reflected at a surface from a point and an incident direction and *Bidrectional Transmittance Distribution Function (BTDF)* describes how light is transmitted through a surface at a point from an incident direction. These can be combined into a encompassing *Bidrectional Scattering Distribution Function (BSDF)*. When consiering only the *BRDF* part, there are a couple main descriptors of this reflectance, namely _diffuse_, _glossy specular_, _perfect specular_ and _retroreflective_.

//   // One of the conventional and most well known diffuse model is *Lambertian Reflectance*. This defines the property that apparent brightness of a surface is equal in all directions. Specifically, it states that materials obey "Lambert's cosine law" where radiant intensity is proportional to cosine of the surface normal and observer's line of sight:
// ]

// $
//   L_("diffuse") = (L_("in") dot N dot L dot R)/pi = (L_("in") dot |N|\|L|cos theta dot R)/pi
// $

// #p(3)[
//   Where $R$ is is the BRDF's reflectance spectrum defining the fraction of incident light that gets scattered. This is divided by $pi$ to ensure total reflectance is equal to $R$ after integrating and to abide by energy conservation. /
// ]

// #p(3)[
//   Another type of BRDF concerns specular reflection which reflects scattered light preferentially in a set of directions. It shows blurry reflections for glossy specular materials and clear reflections for perfect specular materials like mirrors. The models here typically derive from *Cook-Torrance Model* which utilizes "microfacet theory" which models a specular surface as a series of "microfacet" surfaces. The more well known derivative is known as *Torrance-Sparrow Model* which performed a color shift to the Fresnel term and substituted the Normal Distribution for one that has better "tail highlights".
// ]
// $
//   R_s = (D * F * G)/(4 * (N dot V) * (N dot L))
// $

// #p(3)[
//   where:
//   - $D$ is the PDF for the microfacet model describing alignment of microfacets with the _half-vector_ (the vector which would perfectly reflect light onto the viewing angle). In Cook-Torrance this is typically the Beckmann or more popular GGX (Trowbridge-Reitz) distribution.
//   - $F$ is specular Fresnel factor which uses the Fresnel equations to describe how reflectance increases at grazing angles
//   - $G$ is the attenuation term to account for other microfacet masking and self-shadowing
//   - $N$ is surface normal vector, $L$ is incident vector and $V$ is the viewing vector
// ]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Volumetric Scattering
]

#p(3)[
  For Volume Scattering, the *(Integro-Differential) Equations of Transfer* describes how radiance along a beam changes within a medium due to attenuation (absorption/scattering) and emission sources from point $p$ to $p' = p + t omega$:
]

$
  partial/(partial t) L_o (p', omega) = -sigma_t (p', omega) L_i (p', -omega) + sigma_t (p', omega) L_s (p', omega)
$

#p(3)[
  where $sigma_t$ is the attenuation coefficient. The above can be used to find _beam transmittance_ $T_r$ between points $p$ and $p'$ with distance $d$ without accounting for emission or in-scattering:
]

$
  T_r (p arrow p') = e^(-integral^d_0 sigma_t (p + t omega, omega)"d"t)
$

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Delta tracking
]

#p(3)[
  Delta tracking is a method of deciding how a beam travelling through a medium should continue along its path, particularly in a heterogeneous volume, and is the recursive estimation of transmittance until termination via Russian Roulette or until sampled point is past endpoint. A physical interpretation of this can be thought of as randomly deciding whether a ray interacts with a true or ficitious particle at each scattering event.
]

#p(3)[
  As a beam travels through a participating medium in PBRT, the volume has a chance to add its own inherent emission to the beam and the beam enters into one of 3 "scattering events" depending on the majorant scattering coefficient. It uses the null scattering coefficient $sigma_n$ (defined as $sigma_n (x) - sigma_"maj" - sigma_t (x) >= 0$) as well as a majorant scattering coefficient $sigma_"maj" >= sigma_t$ (where $sigma_t = sigma_a + sigma_s$ is the attenuation/transmittance coefficient and $sigma_a$ is the absorption coefficient and $sigma_s$ is the scatterig coefficient) that's defined as an upper bound on the transmittance coefficient everywhere along the ray. Using this $sigma_"maj"$ it can treat the medium as if it were homogeneous and to determine the probability of each scattering event. These 3 scattering events are:
  #list(
    [*Absorption*: the path terminates as if light was absorbed by a particle in the medium and the throughput $beta$ is set to 0 for all sampled wavelengths],
    [*Scattering*: update the throughput tracking variables $beta$ and if throughput is non-zero, enqueue medium scattering work depending on the phase function and also terminates sampling the medium itself. This medium scattering kernel then samples a light source and pushes a shadow ray in order to calculate direct lighting. If it does not sample a light source, it handles indirect lighting by spawning a new ray in a random direction based on phase function with additional russian roulette guards to prevent low contribution paths.],
    [*Null Scattering*: doesn't correspond to a physical phenomena but is a way to more easily sample transmittance in heterogeneous media as if it were homogeneous but in a way that doesn't effect the distribution of light. It fills the media with "fictious particles" which oversaturate the medium such that the volume has a constant majorant density/transmittance $sigma_"maj"$. When a null scattering event occurs, it causes medium sampling to continue, as if it never interacted with this ficitious particle. The probabilities are normalized by this $sigma_"maj"$ to compensate for filling the volume with these particles.],
  )
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Monte Carlo and Multiple Importance Sampling
]

#p(3)[
  PBRT evaluates the light transport equation via *Monte Carlo Integration*, a stochastic method to approximate integrals where analytical solutions are intractable through averaging $N$ random samples drawn from PDF $p(x)$. This yields an unbiased estimator $I approx 1/N sum^N_(i=1) (f(x_i)/p(x_i))$ where $f(x_i)$ is the contribution of sample $x_i$. To minimize the variance, PBRT uses *Multiple Importance Sampling* by drawing samples from distributions closely matching the shape of the integrand (samples that have the highest contribution to the final output). MIS also robustly combines samples from multiple different distributions (emissive volume, scattering phase function etc) to apply optimal statistical weights to each strategy to minimize overall variance.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  PBRT-v4 on the GPU
]

#p(2)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Path tracing typically runs on a GPU as it utilizes the unique architecture and parallelism it offers. However, this requires redesigning the algorithm to accomodate as GPU architectures prioritize throughput, doing many computations in parallel and finishing all as quickly as possible.

  This throughput-first design means devoting less space on the chip for caches and predictive/speculative execution hardware to increase the number of computational units. However, for optimal performance *occupancy* is crucial but requires data to be readily available as memory fetches can result in processor idling for hundreds of cycles. Whilst one thread is waiting for a read to complete, the GPU switches to executing instructions in another thread and if this requests a read, can switch to another and so on. Given enough threads and computation between reads, this keeps the GPU occupied. However, this requires architecting more paralleism to run at peak occupancy and requires tens of thousands of threads to fully utilize. Fortunately, path tracing can leverage this.

]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  GPU Kernels and Wavefronts in PBRT-v4
]

#p(3)[
  Threads on a GPU can be organized or grouped into _execution units_  which execute 32, 64 or 128 threads concurrently. The main execution model centres around a *kernel* runs on the GPU by a specific number of threads. _Launching_ a kernel refers to informing GPU to execute a particular _kernel function_ concurrently. Kernels do not return a value so they must write results into memory before exiting. GPUs also bundle threads into _thread groups_ (of 32 threads) which share a single instruction decoder but means for kernels which have branching, the GPU will execute both with a subset of threads in the group disabled. This, in the worst case, causes a $32times$  loss in performance thus less divergence is better. This is particularly bad in path tracing where in each bounce, there are many different directions a light ray can diverge into. Thus, it is best to separate work into similar paths
]

#p(3)[
  To accomodate this, PBRT-v4 uses a *Wavefront Architecture* where rays are processed in parallel and organized into work queues. A wavefront path tracer separates main operations of the algorithm into separate kernels. For a path tracer, these kernels may generate camera rays, find ray intersections, evaluate surface intersections and so on. These kernels are organized so some kernels produce work items that are enqueued into another. In PBRT-v4, this is implemented in `WavefrontIntegrator` where the architecture is defined as:
]
#align(center)[
  #figure(
    image("Figure 15.2.png", height: 15%),
    caption: [Overview of Kernels Used in `WavefrontIntegrator`, also #link("https://pbr-book.org/4ed/Wavefront_Rendering_on_GPUs/Mapping_Path_Tracing_to_the_GPU#SystemOverview")[Figure 15.2 in PBRT-v4]],
  )
]

// #p(3)[
//   The advantage of this model is that execution in a kernel can start fully converged and whilst it may diverge inside the kernel function, it regularly converges them back in the next kernel. However, a cost of this is memory bandwidth, as data does not persist between launches and kernel reads its inputs from memory and outputs after executing.
// ]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF)
]<nerf_model_section>
#p(2)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF), introduced by Mildenhall et al @mildenhall2020nerfrepresentingscenesneural, emerged as a popular method of training on a specific scene by taking images as input, in order to synthesize novel views across the scene. It gained its popularity for its ability to output high-quality renderings with small training times, on the magnitude of seconds.

  Their approach represents the scene as 5D vectors of spatio-directional coordinates $vb(c) = (x, y, z)$ and $(theta, phi)$, and models their network as a 5D function $F_Theta : (vb(x), vb(d)) -> (vb(c), sigma)$ that predicts $c = (r, g, b)$ and volume density $sigma$ and thus achieves multi-view coherence by restricting input to only be based on this location and view direction and use a fully connected network to model this function. It also employs hierarchical volume sampling, utilizing a coarse and fine network to more efficiently and effectively sample rays in areas which have the highest contributions. Since this approach was only designed for static scenes, further strides have been made to adapt this NeRF approach for more dynamic scenes.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Neural Radiance Caching (NRC)
]

#p(2)[

  Traditional techniques for real-time global illumination have significant issues when dealing with high-frequency details due to the discretization artifacts from spatial hashing structures and irradiance probes and can also have slow update times for dynamic scenes. To resolve this, there have been advancements in improving the speed of path tracing techniques, all without sacrificing quality, in real-time rendering applications.

  Researchers at NVidia @NRC have created a technique called *Neural Radiance Caching (NRC)*, as a way to perform path tracing to improve the realism of global illumination without suffering from drawbacks from traditional techniques. It replaces the prior offline neural rendering techniques with small fully fused Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) networks that are trained online during the rendering process itself. Instead of the previously pretrained networks, which require lengthy training times, these small MLPs are initialized with random weights and learn along side rendering. By leveraging a self-training approach, the network can learn from noisy path-traced samples generated by the previous path vertex, and thus can effectively simulate infinite bounces.  By generalizing via adaptation rather than a pre-learned network, it is robust to dynamic scenes and can simulate changing lighting or geometry. This network also achieves SOTA noise reduction and performance, with an overhead of \~2.6 ms per frame.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Instant Neural Graphics Primitives (Instant-NGP)
]

#p(2)[
  A group of Nvidia researchers @nvidia_instant_ngp developed a new parametric encoding of positions, which helps address some of the performance bottlenecks of pure MLP-based representations (without parametric encodings) called Instant Neural Graphics Primitives (Instant-NGP). This replaces the singular, large and deep MLP of NeRF with a hybrid architecture comprising of a "Multi-resolution hash encoding" network which performs the positional parametric encoding, and a much smaller and faster MLP to infer the final directional emitted color $vb(c)$ and the volume density $sigma$.

  Instead of querying the network for the entire input, this new architecture maps the input coordinates $vb(x) = (x, y, z)$ to a set of trainable feature vectors stored in hash tables of multiple resolution levels. These multi-level features are interpolated and concatenated, together with auxiliary data, to be passed into the smaller MLP. This approach reduces the computational complexity, improving both training and inference time, as the multi-resolution hash table lightens the burden of storing and representing high-frequency spatial detail and allows the smaller MLP to act simply as a decoder. It was also demonstrated to be task-agnostic, meaning it can act as a generic neural graphics primitive.

  The study also goes beyond just comparing against traditional NeRF approaches and also outperforms other techniques such as "Neural Radiance Caching" @NRC, where it learned to cache 5D light field from Monte Carlo path tracer in real-time. The authors note that the architecture's ability to handle hash collisions via SGD; gradients from more "important" samples (surfaces which contribute more to the final luminance) dominate optimization, allowing the network to allocate resources to relevant regions of the volume. This adaptability makes this an ideal architecture for approximating complex, non-uniform scattering functions in participating media.
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  SeaThru NeRF
]

#p(2)[
  While NeRF has revolutionized novel view synthesis for scenes without medium participation, their application to scenes that are underwater or foggy remains challenging due to the added complexity of modelling light interacting with participating medium. To address this, researchers @SeaThru_Nerf have proposed a new architecture, called SeaThru-NeRF, which is an extension of the NeRF model that incorporates an additional network.

  Unlike standard NeRF approaches that conflate surface radiance with path (volume) radiance, their method decomposes the scene into separate "object" and "medium" fields, parameterized by separate neural networks. By learning to separate the medium property values with surface properties without requiring "clear" GT images without the medium interactions, achieves SOTA restoration and depth estimation even in the face of scattering media.
]



#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Methodology and Implementation
]

#p(1)[
  This project will be broken into various phases of work to help narrow down the focus:
  #list(
    [*Dataset Generation*],
    [*Data Visualization and Normalization*],
    [*Validation Testing and Hyperparameter Evaluation*],
    [*Training and Model Visualization*],
    [*Pipeline-Model Integration and Inference*],
  )
]




#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Dataset Generation and Loading
]

#p(2)[
  The first thing was identifying what the model does within the context of the PBRT wavefront architecture. For this project, the model focuses on being able to output data that predicts volumetric values used within the *media scattering* kernel, and thus the inputs to this model are:
  #model-inputs-outputs

  An important note is initially the network was trained with the intent it learns *pure transmittance*, however upon further examination of results and the code, discovered it learned a *weighted throughput ratio* due to *Multiple Importance Sampling* (MIS). A more rigorous explanation is given in `tafter_linear_scaling.typst` file but the gist is that PBRT is a spectral path tracer and thus samples scattering events dependent on the first sampled wavelength. To remain an unbiased estimator, it then divides by the probability of the sampled event to compensate for the other sampled wavelengths. This doesn't change the integration, but does affect some of its wider scale application or eventual results.

  The outputs of this model are the $T$ weighted throughput ratio values of the ray describing the proportion of incident radiance that has been attenuated and $L_e$ the radiance added from volume emission which is scaled for MIS. The other important values that needs to be extracted at the end is calculated from the $beta$ transmission value before the current ray was traced. The reason for that comes from how $beta$ is used in conjunction with $L_e$ in calculating throughput. Firstly, the $beta$ acts as an "accumulated throughput" of the ray along its journey around the scene. In PBRT during medium scattering sampling, it samples $T_"maj"$ of the medium and the radiance emitted is scaled by this $beta$ value. Thus, the $L_e$ output from this kernel is the scaled radiance dependent on the path the ray has taken previously (so light sources and scattering effects have less of an impact on a ray further along the path since it would be "dimmer"). The model must train this on the intrinsic brightness the medium adds to rays irrespective of the rays previous bounces, which requires dividing $L_e$ with the current $beta$ value:

  $
    L_"intrinsic" = (L_e)/beta_"current"
  $

  Similarly, the weighted throughput ratio $T$ will need to be calculated from both the $beta_"current"$ before the sample medium scattering kernel started and the $beta_"new"$ calculated via scaling by $T_"maj"$ as the ray is traced through the medium. Thus to calculate the $T$ weighted throughput ratio of the ray through the medium only, we need to divide the fraction of light that comes out of the medium by the fraction of light that entered it.   During inference, the inverse operation is performed to calculate adjusted emission and throughput values.

  $
    T = beta_("new") / beta_("current")
  $

]

#p(2)[
  The model will train on RGB values due to the way spectral MIS sampling works. In PBRT by default, it performs calculations on 4 sampled wavelengths, sampled depending on the film used, and these 4 sampled wavelengths are sampled randomly for each pixel/sample. Thus, to have a consistent output radiance space for training and due to the previous work being done on RGB values, the values from PBRT are converted to RGB via an `ToOutputRGB` which performs the conversion to RGB radiance and performs a color space transformation to the default standard XYZ color space.
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Training Scene
]

#p(3)[
  The scene trained on was the `explosion.pbrt` scene given in the PBRT-v4 scenes repository as it was a scene that had a non-zero volume emission and transmittance. Here is an image from the default scene render

  #align(center)[
    #image(
      "../images/explosion.png",
      height: 12%,
    )
  ]
]


#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Rendering Multiple Orientations
]


#p(3)[
  In order for the network to learn the scene representation, it is necessary to gather samples generated from multiple orientations around the scene. Thus, PBRT was modified and additional command line arguments were added to specify how many orientations to render in. \

  Though seemingly trivial, it required a large change in PBRT's codebase as the program assumes it will only render from one camera perspective at a time and exits, allowing the OS to reclaim resources. However, for the purposes of the project, rendering from multiple novel orientations is required and thus significant cleanup was required due to the fact that creating resources for new novel views rebuilt certain scenes, materials and Optix structures.

  Once this work was done, camera creation code was modified to generate these novel orientations. It samples positions in the hemisphere around the origin with a radius that is the distance of the camera to the origin and reorientates the camera to look towards the origin. The reason for this is, for the scenes considered for this project, the program only considers volumes which are centered at the origin just for ease of gathering data and also ensures that from whichever positions it sampled, it shoud include the majority of the volume.
]

#p(3)[
  The number of orientations given for each set are the following:
  - Training set: $5$ orientations $approx$ 17GB
  - Validation set: $3$ orientations $approx$ 12GB
  - Test set : $3$ orientations $approx$ 12 GB
]

#p(3)[
  The reason for the above was because due to ensuring they would be able to fit into VRAM/RAM. The GPU available (NVidia A5000) only has about 24GB of VRAM which limited the size of these datasets. However, 17GB of training data should be sufficient.
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Instant-NGP and Data Loading
]

#p(3)[
  The C++/CUDA code in Instant-NGP was chosen to be modified for the project as opposed to the python bindings that are available. There are 2 reasons for this:
  - *Custom data pipeline*: the proposed model is very different from the built in modes in Instant-NGP and the bindings will not provide the flexibility that the lower level CUDA code would.
  - *PBRT integration*: It will be much easier to do the integration in the language that PBRT is written in, as this can leverage the Instant-NGP code to create and load the tiny-cuda-nn model itself.

  Instant-NGP performs all its data loading, model training/inference and model checkpoint loading/saving via a `Testbed` class. Testbed has different modes but due to the custom data pipeline for this project, `Testbed` was extended by adding a new `ETestbedMode::PBRT` value. This also required creating custom loading functions, adding `train_pbrt_prep` and `train_pbrt` functions and adding some member variables to hold tracking dataset properties and hold buffer pointers to the training, validation and test sets. Fortunately, this did not require changing the way the network and kernels were setup, as they are created by specifying a `json` configuration file. A child class was not necessary since it only changes mode based on an enum member variable.

  Initially the loading functions only dealt with training data, but then later handles different types via an enum value. It first loads the file, reads the number of samples and allocates a CPU buffer, copies the data from the file, performs some preparation steps and normalization and then uploads the data to the GPU if there is space whilst keeping track of the number of samples loaded. The specifics are detailed in `testbed.h`, `testbed.cu` and `main_nn.cu`.

  At first all data was eventually loaded into VRAM but during training, it was realized that the test set would not fit simultaneously with training data was. Therefore, this was changed to instead use *pinned CUDA memory* which marks a region of CPU-side memory to be shared with the GPU to allow quicker access and not require it to be loaded into the already full VRAM. This is slightly slower than having it in VRAM but the cost is alleviated by the fact that test data won't be accessed every training iteration and only at a lower set frequency.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Data Preparation and Visualization
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  Data Visualization
]<Data_Visualization>

#p(3)[
