    "../images/training_visualized_graphs/tmax.png",
    columns: 2,
  )
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Data Normalization
]


#p(3)[
  As with any machine learning task, preparing the data is crucial to allow the model to learn anything. Typically, inputs will need to be normalized or mapped to a specific range of values in order to prevent gradients from exploding or vanishing during SGD and optimization. This is the case in Instant-NGP. Below is a table containing information on how the data was normalized:
]

#p(1)[

  #table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: horizon,
    table.header([*Input*], [Input Range], [Normalized Range], [*Output*], [Input Range], [Normalized Range]),
    [
      _Ray Position_ $(x, y, z)$
    ],
    [
      Maximum of scene bounds
    ],
    [
      $[-1,1]$
    ],
    [
      _RGB Radiance_ $L_e (r, g, b)$
    ],
    [
      $[0, "Max float value"]$
    ],
    [
      #text(size: 8pt)[
        $[0,ln("Max" L_e + 1)]$
      ]
    ],

    [
      _Ray Direction_ $(x, y, z)$
    ],
    [
      [-1, 1]
    ],
    [
      [0, 1]
    ],
    [
      _Weighted Throughput Ratio_ $T (r, g, b)$
    ],
    [
      $[0, infinity]$
    ],
    [
      $[0, 1]$
    ],

    [
      `tMax`
    ],
    [
      $[0,$"Max Scene bounds"$]$
    ],
    [
      $[0, 1]$
    ],
  )
]


#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Ray Position
]

#p(4)[
  Instant-NGP, similar to the other modes such as NeRF and Volume, require ray positions to be normalized to between $[-1, 1]$ for even and consistent hashgrid coverage and in fact in `nerf_loader.cu`, it performs this normalization by dividing by the scene AABB. Therefore, during loading, `Testbed` keeps track of the bounds of the scene in order to normalize.
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Ray Direction
]

#p(4)[
  Similarly for Ray directions in other Instant-NGP modes, they require to be normalized. Though, they need to be normalized to be between $[0, 1]$, which is just an API-level choice made by Instant-NGP for consistency.
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  `tMax`
]

#p(4)[
  `tMax` is an auxilliary input, but to prevent the final MLP from being overly reliant on this input and having it dominate, requires normalization as well, which can be done by finding the max value of `tMax` within the dataset and dividing all `tMax` values by this. However, some care needs to take place as in PBRT, tMax could be arbitrarily large for cases of "escaped rays". In this case, according to the scene, `tMax` was clamped to a max value of $160$ which is justified in @Data_Visualization
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Radiance $L_e$
]

#p(4)[
  The outputs for the model need to be carefully normalized for the loss values to meaningfully update the model weights through SGD. However, because of the fact that radiance values themselves can be arbitrarily large due to radiance being able to represent High Dynamic Range (HDR), just doing linear normalization $L/L_"max"$ will preserve extreme sparsity of the emission distribution and thus dimmer parts of the explosion would be overshadowed by parts with extreme radiance emission. /*so after the linear scaling the less emitting smoke would have a value of $10^(-3)$ and the core would be $1.0$.*/ Thus the network will overfit to those higher peaks and ignore the lower-intensity radiances.

  Instead, logarithmic scaling was employed by transforming radiance values into $y=ln(L + 1)$. This ensures the loss function minimizes relative error instead of absolute, ensuring that the network has enough capacity to learn both high and low emission as opposed to higher emission dominating. It also keeps the output range to be in a similar magnitude as Transmittance to ensure it doesn't dominate. The $+1$ term ensures stability for areas that don't have emission.
]

#heading(depth: 4)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  Weighted Throughput Ratio $T$
]



#p(4)[
  Weighted throughput ratio is normalized to $[0, 1]$ as it is the fraction of light that transmits through. However, due to the conversion to RGB and the calculations needed to isolated volume transmittance within one ray sampling step in `media.cpp`,
  // ```cpp
  // Float pr = T_maj[0] * mp.sigma_s[0];
  // beta *= T_maj * mp.sigma_s / pr;
  // .
  // .
  // .
  // SampledSpectrum T_target = beta / beta_before;
  // ```
  causes some $T$ values to have a transmittance value $>1$. For example, if the path already had a $beta=0.5$ and then the beta after was calculated to be 0.8 then the overall transmittance value is calculated to be $>1$. This may not only cause divergence in training, but also doesn't make physical sense.

  Initially, this was clamped to between $[0, 1]$ as it was assumed that the `SampleMediumInteraction` function logged pure transmittance and thus any transmittance logged that was larger than $1$ was physically impossible, but with the new knowledge, it makes sense for values to be more than 1 to compensate. More details are in `tAfter_linear_scaling.typst` file. Instead a *linear scaling* is performed. This scaling is then kept in `m_volume_training_tAfter_scale` variable which is used when loading validation and test data as well as inference preparation.

  There are a few consequences of the network learning weighted throughput ratio values. Firstly, this network architecture only works for spectral MIS path tracing. Secondly, since there is a conversion to RGB via an `ToOutputRGB` call which performs tonemapping and readjustment dependent on colourspace, there is some bias and will potentially look different for different scene configurations of sensor models or colorspaces used, since it does not learn pure transmittance.

  Furthermore, due to the `ToOutputRGB`, some useful data will inevitably be lost as there are 4 wavelengths considered compared to just the 3 RGB outputs and thus may overadjust the green to have larger values (as its spectrum is generally wider than red and blue) so the inferred image will be biased due to this as well as the network architecture potentially not being fit for scenes that have a much higher number of sampled wavelengths.
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Validation Testing and Hyperparameter Evaluation
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Validation Testing and Batching Strategy
]

#p(3)[
  A batching method for the custom data pipeline for this model was required to be made. It is impractical to have the model train on the entire train set every iteration and there is evidence in previous studies (@nvidia_instant_ngp and @mildenhall2020nerfrepresentingscenesneural) that training on a randomized large batch is sufficient so the validation testing uses minibatch-SGD to perform both training and validation testing using a batch size of $2^19$ with replacement done via a GPU kernel which randomly indexes the training/validation test set. Each run trains the model for 50,000 iterations and every 5,000 iterations it calculates the validation error with a final validation error calculated on the whole set at the end.

  However, one important thing to note is that the due to the size of both the train and validation set, they both cannot fit in memory at the same time, therefore the ongoing validation error was calculated on a fixed size of $2^19$ samples. The reason is because it allows seeing the general trend of the validation error over time as opposed to a final validation error that may be going down but not the lowest error among the configurations.
]


#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Hyperparameter setup
]

#p(3)[
  The main hyperparameters to test for are hyperparameters regarding the multi-resolution hashgrid, the Fully Fused MLP and the learning rate. This was tested by running validation testing on each hyperparameter, just slightly different to the default configuration given in `default_config.json`. This default configuration is based off of the configuration that gave the best results in the Instant NGP paper @nvidia_instant_ngp. Below is a table of different configuration values tested given below
]

#align(center)[
  #set text(size: 8.5pt)
  #table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: horizon,
    table.header([*Hyperparameter*], [Values Tested], [*Hyperparameter*], [Input Range]),
    [
      FF MLP `n_neurons`
    ],
    [
      32, 64, 128
    ],
    [
      FF MLP `n_hidden_layers`
    ],
    [
      2, 3, 4
    ],

    [
      Hashgrid `n_levels`
    ],
    [
      20, 16, 12, 8, 4, 2, 1
    ],

    [
      Hashgrid `log2_hashmap_size`
    ],
    [
      21, 19, 17, 15, 13, 11
    ],
    [
      Hashgrid `baseres`
    ],
    [
      32, 24, 16
    ],

    [
      Spherical Harmonics Degree
    ],
    [
      6, 4, 2
    ],

    [
      `tMax` `nFreq`
    ],
    [
      4, 6, 8
    ],
    [
      Learning rate $eta$
    ],
    [
      $1^(-1), 5^(-2), 1^(-2), 5^(-3), 1^(-3), 5^(-4), 1^(-4)$
    ],

    [
      Loss Function
    ],
    [
      L2, L1, RelativeL2
    ],
  )
]




#p(3)[
  The FF MLP hyperparameters detail how large the final inference MLP is whereas the Hashgrid detail how large the hashgrid that learns a scene representation and which generates feature vectors from the input position. Larger `n_levels` values means more hashgrid resolutions which can lead to more collisions but also a finer, more detailed representation of the scene. `log2_hashmap_size` details the size of the hashmap used to get the feature vector values once the positions in the grid have been calculated. So a larger hashmap size, the more feature vectors available. The hashgrid `baseres` parameter details the size of the base resolution of the first level of the hashgrid is which plays with the `per_level_scale` which determines how much more fine (how many bins in each dimension) the hashgrid for the next level up has. Finally, the Spherical Harmonics Degree and the `nFreq` detail how those values are encoded as directional/auxilliary data inputs.
]


#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Validation Testing Results
]

#image-grid(
  "../imgs/ffmlp_validation.png",
  "../imgs/nlevels_validation.png",
  "../imgs/mapsizes_validation.png",
