#p(3)[
  To summarize the results,  the validation error on the smaller hashgrid and FF MLP hyperparameters was better than the validation error on larger hashgrid and FF MLP hyperparameters as you can see a lot of the PSNR stayed around 28 dB. Furthermore, the hyperparameters for the SHdegree and `nFreq` didn't have much of a difference but did affect the time taken to train slightly. It also listed a lower learning rate of about $1^(-3)$ to be stabler than higher ones but large enough to learn/converge quickly. The final "chosen" configuration based off of the validation testing is detailed in `validation_based_config.json`. However, as will be seen in the next section, these results will appear misleading. Notes and a more detailed discussion about this is given in various `typst` files, but  prominently in `validation_error_explanation.typst`


]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Training and Model Visualization
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Model Training
]

#p(3)[
  For training, the model was trained for 100,000 iterations on the chosen configurations, with test error calculated every 10,000 iterations on the test set. As opposed to validation testing, the entire test set was used to calculate the test error. Below is a graph of the test error for `validation_based_config.json` as well as a couple other graphs that will be discussed in the next section:
  // "../imgs/test_mse.png",
  #image-figure(
    "../imgs/test_psnr.png",
    "../images/medium_large_testerror.png",
    "../images/spectral_testerror.png",
    columns: 3,
    height: 12%,
  )
]



#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Model Visualization and Issues
]

#p(3)[
  The results from the validation testing section indicated a much lower network capacity would generalize better. However, the results can be misleading (explained in `cyan_background.typst`, `network_fidelity_and_radiance_issues.typst` and `validation_error_explanation.typst`). Another method to verify what was learned and how well the model learned is to visualize slices of the scene to see how well the hashgrid learnt the scene itself. This is relatively common, especially in networks which have some sort of encoding. Functionality was added to take z-slices (horizontal slices since PBRT uses Z-up) of the whole scene's bounds via `dump_slice_img`.

  #flame_visualizations<flame_visualizations>

  The images for the validation configuration look very blocky and inconsistent between slices and this is a consequence of the network being small itself. This would imply a much larger network would be better to learn from as it would have more space to be able to learn a finer representation of the scene, evidenced by the above. However, this comes with its own challenges with learning the RGB radiance in larger networks, also shown above but also the fact that some images appeared completely black in the appendix, likely due to overfitting and dead ReLU. All of which is explained in `network_fidelity_and_radiance.typst`.


]

#p(3)[
  However, you can see that the general shape for both radiance and the throughput is similar to the actual shape of the explosion, with an expanding and contracting circular shape which would represent the fire/smoke and these slices are also consistent with each other in consecutive slices which means that the network itself is actually learning the medium's transmittance as well as the radiance.

  Furthermore, you can also see that in places which did not have much training data that there is some random noise. This makes sense for areas devoid of training data to push gradients towards learning that space. This is not inherently a bad thing as this could mean the spectral path tracer does not trace those areas and so does not need representing.

  One final note is for some network configurations smaller than `medium.json` (e.g. `magenta.json`), there are further strange radiance issues, such as some appearing magenta or even green. This is explored and explained in `network_fidelity_and_radiance_issues.typst`.

  The throughput was also visualized and given in the appendix. For the most part, the shapes also match very well and can even see in some an enveloping circle representing the bounding sphere. However, there is a cyan background for some, and this is explained in `cyan_background.typst`.
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Pipeline-Model Integration and Inference
]

#p(2)[
  The integration required considering how exactly the Instant-NGP `Testbed` functionality is included, either via directly adding as a member variable or adding a smaller amount of functionality for `tiny-cuda-nn` instead. The exact details are given in `pbrt_integration.typst`, where it describes why `Testbed` was added as a member variable and other specific details on how exactly the network was integrated and how the data was prepared and unpacked.

  `SampleMediumInteractionNGP`, called if a network is loaded and NGP is specified to be ran, extracts work items from `MediumInteractionWorkItem` queue and prepares them into a staging buffer to be inferred through the model. The predictions are then extracted and used to update the `pixelSampleState` or passes on the calculated `beta` values by enqueuing work in the proceeding kernels.

  Fortunately, other than initializing staging buffers `r_l`, `r_u` and `beta` before/after and `lambda` before/after inference buffers to pass through certain values, all the changes required are local to `SampleMediumInteractionNGP` and don't require change in other areas. This is due to the wavefront architecture design leading to each kernel function being independent and isolated from the others allowing the integration to be isolated only to `SampleMediumInteraction`

  However, under the assumption that the network can learn the scattering itself, it assumes the ray passes through the entire `tMax` distance into the medium and either leaves or hits another medium. Thus no medium scattering kernel called and so no `MediumScatteringWorkItem` is enqueued from the new `SampleMediumInteractionNGP` unlike the native way. There are also a couple side effects of this. Firstly, if the network detects emission in front of the smoke, then it will cause it to skip the smoke behind it and not have dark non-transmittive cloud behind it and the opposite is true, if the network detects non-emitting low transmittance smoke in front of the emitting fire, it will not output any emission at all as it skips straight through. Further exploration is given in `radiance_transmission_slice_mismatch.typst`. Another consequence of this volume skip through is that any shadow rays that would have been enqueued during `SampleMediumScattering` does not occur so indirect light or self-shadowing of the volume cannot occur. Overall, there are quite a few limitations that need to be considered during the evaluation of the model and the inference results.
]




#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Results and Evaluation
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Results
]<Results>

#p(2)[
  Below are results comparing the 3 metrics. Note, this includes spectral network results that are explained below in @Ablation_Study
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Visual Quality
]<Visual_Quality_Section>

#all-views-figure

#p(3)[
  Note for the above images other than Large Color configuration, only throughput was taken for these images, due to the issues visualizing radiance and other reasons given in `radiance_transmission_slice_mismatch.typst`. There may be some way to ensure the radiance is rendered regardless of whether the network thinks its in front or behind the network. However this project will only consider a relatively simple integration of the model into PBRT as this would require significant divergence from the original algorithm. The throughput and radiance mismatch may also be alleviated with a higher fidelity hashgrid, but this comes with its own problems (see `network_fidelity_and_radiance_issues.typst`). Thus, throughput would be a better comparison as well the fact that it serves as a better way of seeing whether the networks learnt the shape of the volume.

  In terms of visual quality there has been partial success. Firstly, the radiance inference for the RGB networks was poor. It failed to show any meaningful yellow/orange flame and this issue was caused by a number of factors from RGB bias from spectral conversion as well as limitations in integration and the impact of the volume scattering skip. When looking at the slice visualizations however, there is evidence to suggest that it did learn the shape of the fire and emissive sections of the scene but this could not be showcased sufficiently in the inference.

  When considering throughput in the larger networks, though not perfect, they did a better job at learning the general shape of the smoke and the finer details and there is an explicit outline of the smoke that matches the original at least for the original/principal view, though there is a lot of noise elsewhere. /*This can be explained via the RGB bias given in `RGB_bias.typst` leading to empty space being learnt to have low throughput areas for red.*/ Smaller `n_levels` networks learned which areas generally had lower throughput but failed to learn the higher frequency areas like the smaller offshoots and wisps of smoke near the edges.

  However, when considering novel views, the network does poorly. This may likely be due to the fact that the training dataset may have had more bias towards directions in the original directions or rays hitting empty space or the bounding volume sphere and thus failed to learn other perspectives. Part of it was also explained in `validation_error_explanation.typst` where a lot of rays just hit the outer sphere instead of the smoke.

  For spectral networks, it was more successful. It was able to get a high quality reconstruction of the original view and in some ways performed better with less grainy and better blended wisps on the edges. On novel views there were some which were poor but others which it did well in reconstructing likely only let down by the training data.
]


#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Computation Time
]

#p(3)[
  #timings-graph

  The computation time is calculated on Max inference batch size of 780000 with padded max batch of 780032 and averaged over 3 runs for all 3 novel views shown in @Visual_Quality_Section.  The computation time is on average $3times$ faster for the NGP route as opposed to the native rendering approach. The above table showcases this. For the native PBRT path, it took approximately 3 seconds over 3 different runs as opposed to the NGP route which took only about 1 second over 3 different renders. This is staggeringly faster and showcases, even via a relatively simple and naive integration, it has massive time saves.
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Memory Usage
]

#p(3)[
  In terms of memory, the network itself does not require that much memory, only on the order of magnitude of $approx 10 "MB"$. However, what takes up more space is the naive implementation of the integration via the multiple staging buffers which are max queue size. Below showcases the calculated amount of extra memory required for different configurations


  You can see the factors that have the most impact on memory usage are tcnn activation memory. However, if utilizing JIT fusion, the costs for that section drops by about 75% (only need to consider tcnn input buffers for the network)

  #memory-table
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Ablation study: Spectral-based Networks
]<Ablation_Study>

#p(3)[
  When considering the performance of the RGB-based networks, this begs the question: Is there a fundamental issue with learning just RGB radiance values? An argument can be made that converting from the higher dimensionality of 4 spectrum values that PBRT samples to just 3 RGB values inherently loses some information. This is due to the conversion both accounting for sensor and RGB colorspace conversion, inherently making the network tied to whichever colorspace and sensor it was trained on, but also due to the fact that many wavelengths for green overlap with red and blue as it attempts to simulate how the cones in human eyes work, potentially giving a heavier bias towards green without taking into account particular sensor sensitivity.

  Thus, an experiment can be conducted to see the results of training only throughput using the 4 spectral throughput values instead of 3 RGB values. However, due to the nature of the network not guaranteeing the same 4 wavelengths sampled during volume scattering/medium interactions, this requires a modification. To conduct this ablation study, PBRT was changed to predictably sample the same 4 wavelengths in order to be deterministic about the wavelengths. Future work could be done to have the specific wavelengths logged and used as input to the network as well, but for now we will consider only the given 4 wavelengths. Unfortunately, without the foresight to also record the position of the camera in the random orientations for the original training dataset, this network was trained on the original position and 4 other random orientations different from the training set. So potentially not a 1-1 comparison but still gives a good way to evalute spectral network performance.

  The configuration used is given in `spectral.json` which is similar to the larger configurations used earlier as those were the ones that gave the best reconstruction but also because this spectral network is not trained on radiance which is what larger models struggled with. The results are given above.
]

#p(3)[
  As you can see, for the original view it had an almost perfect reconstruction, matching even the smaller and finer wisps of smoke that the original had. Compared to the original, the wispier parts appear less grainy/noisy and does a better job at smoothly interpolating from higher to lower throughput compared to the native approach which is expected from a neural network as it tends to prefer a smoother interpolation/function to learn. Overall, for the original/principal view, it does a very good job at reconstruction.

  However, looking at the novel orientations, it appears there are issues but not as bad as the RGB network version. The first novel view is pretty much a mess with only a very small number of pixels having the correct throughput and mostly looks like noise, but this can be attributed to the volume skip and the bounding sphere in the scene. The second novel view did a lot better. Though there were quite a few areas which had a throughput of 1, not matching the original, the overall shape matched and had the same distinct outline of the smoke. This could have been a limitation of the size or variety of the training set but this second view shows potential.
]

#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Reflection and Discussion of Future Work
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Critical Reflection
]

#p(2)[
  // Looking back at the results we can break down how successful the this project has been across the 3 metrics proposed in Section 1.3
  Looking back at the metrics given in @Project_Objectives and the results given in @Results, this project has achieved its project objectives.
  #list(
    [*Visual Quality*: results show this metric was good in areas which the training data had sufficient coverage of and could generate novel views to at least a recognizable state with the principal view having arguably better quality than the native path.],
    [*Computation Time*: This metric was the clear winner, with all configurations being approximately $3 times$ faster than the native path, indicating that the model shows promise and success on this front],
    [*Memory Usage*: The results show that larger networks including the spectral version, seem to have a high memory usage, but considering the composition of the extra memory used, the majority of this memory usage is in the tcnn activation buffers and not the networks themselves which are very small, only approximately $34"MB"$ max. In fact, the tcnn activation buffers can be significantly reduced by enabling JIT fusion which performs optimizations of fusing the network kernels together. Therefore with this setting enabled, the majority of the cost is in the inference staging buffers used in the integration. Thus, with a better strategy for integration and with some better optimization, it is likely the memory costs can be reduced even further, but this is likely for future work to consider],
  )

  If this project were to be done again, with the insight gathered from these results, it would be more advantageous to give a bigger consideration of exactly how to deal with the `SampleMediumScattering` kernel, whether it should be better integrated into the network's responsibilities or how to deal with the volume being skipped with this current architecture, as it is clear the network learned the radiance and throughput, but the translation in the integration was suboptimal. Other areas which could have more had more work was the training set creation. Similarly to how Monte Carlo estimation tries to match samples with areas of higher importance, the training set could put more precedence on those higher frequency areas, as opposed to the less prominent areas like how the bounding sphere volume gave issues during validation and novel view synthesis. It could have used a much larger training set too, with more uniform orientations of the scene. The lack of more powerful GPUs would have hindered the size of the datasets utilized during the lifetime of the project.

]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Future Work
]

#p(2)[
  Due to the deficiencies that resulted from performing the RGB conversion from the spectral path tracer, future work should focus on training on spectral data and particuarly looking at utilizing spectral networks for the radiance as it looks like it may be able to perform well to predict radiance as well as the weighted throughput ablation study seems to do well. Furthermore, more work should be done to look at a better strategy for integration, specifically about how to consider compensating for the loss of the medium scattering kernel/call or even about how to potentially incorporate that into the network as well which may require a different approach to take into account the non-deterministic nature of Monte Carlo estimation. This may be in the form of another Fully Fused MLP that runs along side or after the MLP that operates on calculating medium interaction. The medium interaction network can then be modified to output positions and event scattering data that is fed into the scattering network to predict the radiance/throughput for those scattered positions/events. In this way, the medium interaction network is more closely aligned with what the original kernel does which is predict scattering events whereas the scattering network actually calculates the effects of those scattering events. This allows for the learned radiance to accurately and correctly be applied in the tracing that could not have occurred with the proposed network in this project. However this would require signficant modification to Instant-NGP in order to train an architecture like this. Another thing that may be useful to consider is adding additional inputs for the specific wavelengths sampled, to account for the fact that native spectral path tracing operates on sampled wavelengths that may not be consistent between pixel samples.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Conclusion
]

#p(2)[
  Overall, the project has had its up and downs but was partially successful in achieving the project objectives. The project was able to successfully build a hybrid pipeline, train a neural network, complete a neural network integration, though rudimentary and with many areas to improve upon, and able to evaluate the performance across 3 metrics. The viability of neural networks in spectral path tracing has also been shown to have some promise. There have points raised which would improve this work, particularly on the integration, and there is promise that pursuing spectral networks, increasing their complexity or becoming more specialized may result in better renderings and should be investigated in future work.
]



